    "result_text": "Done. The build is green.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe spec adds a derived `riskFlag` boolean to the owner response, true when **any** of three conditions hold. Two of the three inputs are *already computed* in `RespondWithOwner.service` \u2014 `possibleDuplicateOf` and `capacityWarning` \u2014 so I reused them rather than recomputing.\n\n**Two files touched:**\n\n1. `src/main/resources/openapi.yml` \u2014 declared the `riskFlag` schema field on the owner DTO (generates `getRiskFlag`/`setRiskFlag`), mirroring the existing `capacityWarning`/`possibleDuplicate` derived fields.\n\n2. `.../rest/function/owner/RespondWithOwner.java` \u2014 one new line in `service` sets the flag by OR-ing the two already-computed signals with a new disposable-adjacent check, plus a small private `disposableAdjacent(email)` helper.\n\n## Mapping to the spec\n- **possible duplicate** \u2192 `possibleDuplicateOf != null` (reuses `PossibleDuplicate`).\n- **city over soft capacity** \u2192 `capacityWarning` (reuses `CapacityWarning`, the 40\u201349 approaching-limit flag).\n- **email domain disposable-adjacent** \u2192 domain equals a known disposable domain or is a subdomain of one (`endsWith(\".\" + d)`). Exact disposable domains are rejected at create by `NormalizeOwnerEmail`, so on a saved owner this catches the subdomain \"adjacent\" cases that slip through.\n\n## Structural impact\nI kept `RespondWithOwner`'s only orchestration method simple (the risk logic is a single expression delegating to the small helper), reused two existing computations instead of adding new pipeline steps, and touched no other classes. The acceptance test (`riskFlag` false for a normal structured owner) and the full suite pass.",
